> But do you avoid killing Izchak?

Sparing Izchak is, in fact, analogous to taking notes to protect
against amnesia. Both involve meta-gaming. To a role-played Nethack
character, Izchak wouldn't necessarily be any more holy than any other
shopkeeper, since the motivation for not killing him - that a late
Devteam member gave his name to this particular shopkeeper - has
absolutely zilch to do with anything inside the game.

I think Douglas made an excellent point, and I believe anyone would be
hard-pressed to show much role-playing going on at all among the
majority of Nethack players. Roguelikes such as ADOM lend themselves
much better to role-playing. Would you skip Sokoban because your wimpy
character didn't feel like pushing around boulders? These
considerations hardly, if ever, enter into most player's decision
making strategies.

Frankly, I don't see why note-taking is considered any more abusive
than reading spoilers; in a sense, the notes you take are a spoiler
accurate only for that game, and the spoiler is titled "Item Identities
in Nethack Game #1633." If, hypothetically, all items always had the
same description, would using such a spoiler, now accurate for all
games, be considered 'undue abuse'?



